WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.110 align:start position:0%
 
perky  modern yet timeless  the mini has been a mainstay in the  sporty subcompact segment for decades  but even classic has to go with the  times at present there are seven  variants of bmw's little grocery getter  the latest version is a refinement of  the standard three-door model it's grown  a bit of course  together with germany's automobile  association adac we tested the five-door  mini cooper hatch  the ada sees klaus britta wonders if  comfort is given way to convenience  the typical go-kart feeling is still  noticeable in the five-door model owing  to the very direct and tight steering  and the stiff suspension  on long rides that results in a loss of  comfort  the engine sounds quite pithy but also a  bit loud in the interior  that's no wonder because the car is  powered by a 1.5 litre turbocharged  gasoline engine with only three  cylinders  100 kilowatts and a maximum torque of  some 220 newton meters is not a bad  rating for the little engine that could  the mini cooper sprints from 0 to 100  kilometers per hour in 8.2 seconds  swerving to avoid sudden obstacles at 90  kilometers per hour doesn't phase the  mini at all and neither does slamming on  the brakes at 100 kilometers per hour  the mini comes to a standstill after a  respectable 34 meters  small car small engine equals a small  bill at the pump that formula actually  works here  using the adac ecotest criteria the new  mini cooper consumes some 5.5 liters of  premium per 100 kilometers the  manufacturer had actually promised 4.7  the workmanship of the mini's interior  is flawless  the design is a bit playful yet lots of  soft plastic belies and effort at high  quality  passengers now have a lot more space in  the rear seat which thanks to the two  extra doors is easily accessible  the cargo area really is mini even for a  mini with a variable volume from 185 to  445 liters  the all-round visibility is excellent  important for city commutes  even with five doors the mini is an  attractive car that will certainly find  a fan base  though it's a little tight getting in  the two rear doors make day-to-day use  much more practical at twenty thousand  six hundred euros our test vehicle cost  just seven hundred euros more than the  three-door model  a worthy investment especially since  driving performance is on par with its  little brother

